Introduction to XFaceMaker/Win
	
	XFaceMaker/Win is the Microsoft Windows extension to XFaceMaker.
	
	The goal of XFaceMaker/Win is to provide a solution for porting
	Motif applications to Microsoft Windows platforms.
	
	The principle of XFaceMaker/Win is the following:
	
	- User interfaces are built as usual with XFaceMaker from standard
	Motif components. A "verification mode" can be activated so that
	you are warned when you use non-portable features of Motif or X.
	
	- XFaceMaker/Win is then used to translate the Motif interface to
	Microsoft Windows source code.
	
	This approach lets you translate existing interfaces, provided that
	they do not use non-portable features.
	
	XFaceMaker/Win translates the whole interface, i.e. the interface
	layout as well as the behaviour programmed as FACE scripts. If the
	interface is portable, the Windows application generated by
	XFaceMaker/Win can be immediately compiled with Microsoft Visual C++.
	
	The code produced is based on the Microsoft Foundation Classes:
	an XmPushButton is translated to a CButton object, etc. For objects
	and functionalities that do not exist in Windows, XFaceMaker/Win
	produces calls to a runtime library, xfmwin.dll. This library
	implements objects such as XmForms.
	
	The library also contains Windows versions of many Motif and
	XFaceMaker functions, so most FACE scripts will be translated
	without modifications.
	
	XFaceMaker/Win version 1.0 handles approximately 35% or all the
	Motif functions and resources. Of course, the portable functions
	and resources have been chosen among the most commonly used, so
	that, for most applications, more than 90% of the resources and
	functions used will be portable.
	
	The code of the application functions can be ported in two ways:
	
	- application functions can be fully ported to Windows; for
	this the user code preservation feature of XFaceMaker can be used
	with application files generated by XFaceMaker;
	
	- XFaceMaker/Win applications can also work in client/server mode:
	the graphical user interface runs on Windows, and issues calls to
	a special version of the Unix application through a network socket for
	application functions; this client/server mode enables you to test
	very quickly your Windows interface without porting your whole
	application to Windows.
	
What XFaceMaker/Win Can Do
	
	XFaceMaker/Win is able to translate completely many complicated
	Motif interfaces, including specific functionalities like XmForms,
	Motif custom dialog boxes, etc.
	
	The XFaceMaker/Win runtime library implements more than 200 Motif or
	FACE functions, and more than 100 resources usable in FACE scripts.
	This means that even complex dynamic interactions will be fully
	translated.
	
	Of course, XFaceMaker/Win does not handle 100% of the huge number
	of fancy possibilities offered by the Motif toolkit. Effort has been
	focused on translating the most standard combinations: non-standard
	interfaces will probably require modifications to be ported to Windows.
	
	
	XFaceMaker/Win translates all the basic features of Motif interfaces:
	
	- all Motif classes, including Forms, RowColumns, ScrolledWindows,
	PushButton, ScrollBars, Scales, PanedWindows, etc.
	
	- visual attributes: named colors, fonts, labels, pixmaps, etc.
	
	- attachments in Forms.
	
	- Motif allbacks are translated to Windows message handlers.
	
	- FACE language features such as dynamic arrays and dynamic typing
	are fully supported.
	
	- active values are fully supported.
	
	- SelectionBoxess and MessageBoxes are translated to dialog boxes
	conforming to the Windows style.
	
	- OptionMenus are translated to ComboBoxes.
	
	
	Among the things that will not be translated correctly:
	
	- translations: XFaceMaker/Win translates only a limited number
	of events in translations tables: key press, key release, button
	press and button release; other translations will be ignored;
	
	- dynamic creation of panes in PanedWindows;
	
	- font assignments in FACE scripts.
	
	XFaceMaker/Win now supports the XRT/graph widget. Interfaces containing
	XRT/graph widgets will be translated to Windows applications using
	MFC-based version of the XRT/graph for Windows.
	
Building Portable Interfaces
	
	To build an interface that you wish to port to Windows, or to test
	the portability of an existing interface, check the item
	"Verify MS Windows Portability" in the "Options" menu. Once this
	option is set, XFaceMaker will verify that you have not used
	non-portable functions or resources, and if you do, warnings will
	be issued.
	
	XFaceMaker tests the following things:
	
	- build resources, i.e. widget resources set through the Resource
	Window while building the interface (some resources can have default
	values that will cause a warning: you will have to change their value
	to remove the warning);
	
	- functions called in FACE scripts;
	
	- resources read or written in FACE scripts with the ':' operator.
	
	The resources and functions allowed while building portable interfaces
	are listed in the XFaceMaker/Win documentation.
	
Generating your Windows Application
	
	(NOTE: If you use Microsoft Visual C++ Version 2.0, you must set the
	environment variable XFM_WINDOWS_MSVC_VERSION to 2 before translating
	your interface. You can use the XFaceMaker Environment box for this)
	
	To generate your Windows application, use the command "Save MS Windows
	Files" in the "File" menu. This will call the XFaceMaker/Win translator
	in the background and display its output in the "Make Appli Output" box.
	
	Once the application files have been generated, they will be converted
	to DOS format and transferred to your PC, provided that you have configured
	the approriate environment variable as explained in
	"Configuring XFaceMaker/Win".
	
	You can then compile your application, provided that you have installed
	the XFaceMaker/Win libraries and includes files on your PC.
	See "Installing the XFaceMaker/Win Runtime".
	
	To compile your application, start Microsoft Visual C++, open
	the Makefile (it is the file with a ".mak" suffix), and select the command
	"Project/Build <name>.exe". Once the application is compiled, launch it
	with "Debug/Go".
	
Installing the XFaceMaker/Win Runtime
	
	Before you can compile an XFaceMaker/Win application on a PC, you
	must first install the XFaceMaker/Win libraries and include files on
	the PC. To do this:
	
	- take the floppy disk shipped with XFaceMaker/Win
	if you have received a distribution on tape, or take the XFaceMaker/Win
	CD-ROM if you have it;
	
	- copy the file "xfmwin.exe" to an empty directory
	on your PC, and execute it; "xfmwin.exe" is a self-extracting archive,
	and it will create other files;
	
	- then, execute the file "install.exe",	which will actually install
	the XFaceMaker/Win files on your PC; "install.exe" will also setup
	Microsoft Visual C++ so that it can find the XFaceMaker/Win libraries
	and include files.
	
Configuring XFaceMaker/Win
	
	XFaceMaker/Win can transfer automatically the Windows source files to
	a PC after they have been generated. For this, you need to set the
	following environment variables (use the command "Environment" in the
	"Windows" menu to map the environment box). At a minimum, you must set
	the XFMWIN_PROTOCOL and XFMWIN_PC_NAME variables.
	
	XFMWIN_PROTOCOL: the protocol you want to use to transfer files; use
	the value "SMB" to use the native Windows protocol, which requires no
	additional software (the other possible value is "NFS" if you have
	a PC-NFS product installed on your PC: see the XFaceMaker/Win
	documentation).
	
	XFMWIN_PC_NAME: the network name of your PC.
	
	XFMWIN_PC_PART: the partition where you want to copy your source files;
	the default is "C".
	
	XFMWIN_PC_HOME: the base directory where your files will be copied; for
	example, if your set this variable to "joe" and if your working directory
	on Unix is "/home/joe/test", the files will be copied to the directory
	"C:\joe\test"; the default is the root directory of the partition.
	
	XFMWIN_PC_DIR: you can use this variable to specify a destination directory
	with a name different from your Unix working directory. For example, setting
	this variable to "test2" will cause XFaceMaker/Win to copy your files to
	"C:\joe\test2".
	
